Merge back the records the scheduled jobs fetched during the outage window

Context: on 2026-10-22 the derived dataset was rolled back to 3a2716b (the last
state verified end to end). This restores the *collected* material that the
12 days of CI runs had produced, without bringing back the corrupted derived
artifacts.

First, a correction to what I reported earlier: the raw response archive was
never lost. `git checkout 3a2716b -- data/` restores tracked paths from that tree
but does not delete files added later, so all 19 responses fetched between
2026-10-12 and 2026-10-22 were still present and were included in the recovery
commit. What had fallen behind were the two accompanying indices.

What this commit merges:

  data/raw/manifest.jsonl   99 -> 118 entries (now documents those 19 files;
                            the archive and its manifest were out of sync)

  data/raw_records.json     88 -> 123 records. CI had only 41 and had lost all
                            48 Tōuritsu records (which is why zh_tw titles went
                            to 0), but it also held genuinely new material:
                              31 works from コミック百合姫 2026年12月号
                               4 new-volume announcements (e.g. 私の推しは悪役令嬢。(13))
                               4 official news items
                            Neither side is a superset, so the merge is a union.

Merge method: deduplicated by the full record content minus `retrieved_at`.
The first attempt keyed on work_url and collapsed 88 records to 59 — several
works share one magazine page URL, and the Tōuritsu records are scraped per
list page, so that key silently merged distinct works and dropped zh_tw titles
from 15 to 1. Content fingerprinting cannot do that.

Honest note on effect: this does NOT change what the site displays. Rebuilding
leaves all 835 works identical in every substantive field — the December issue
features the same series as November, so serialization status, issue labels and
volume counts are unchanged. The value is that the December issue is now in the
record store, so the next genuine fetch has it, and the raw archive's manifest
matches its contents.

Also: build_dataset.py now preserves an existing created_at per work id instead
of resetting it to NOW on every rebuild. created_at means "when this record was
first created", not "when the dataset was last rebuilt"; resetting it turned every
rebuild into a ~21,000-line diff of pure timestamps that buried real changes.

Verified after: 835 works (747 manga + 88 light novels), 15 zh_tw titles,
298 anime, 0 schema errors, raw_records counts self-consistent, all acceptance
gates and the web verify suite pass.
